{\rtf1\ansi\deff0{\fonttbl{\f0\fnil Calibri;}}\viewkind4\uc1\pard\fs28\b Azure Databricks Fundamentals and My CDC Data Engineering Project\b0\par\par\pard\fs22\b 1. What this project is about\b0\par This project is a real-time data engineering pipeline built on Azure Databricks. It reads database changes from MySQL, sends them through Kafka, and stores the data into Databricks Delta tables using a lakehouse architecture.\par\par The goal is to:\par - capture changes from a source system in near real time\par - ingest them into Kafka\par - process them in Databricks\par - store the data in Delta tables\par - create clean and business-ready layers\par - ensure quality and schema stability\par - automate the pipeline through jobs and deployment tooling\par\par This project follows a production-style data engineering pattern used in real enterprise systems.\par\par\pard\fs22\b 2. What the project does in simple words\b0\par The project listens to MySQL database changes using Debezium. Debezium monitors the MySQL binary log and detects when rows are inserted, updated, or deleted.\par\par These changes are converted into JSON events and sent to Kafka. Then Databricks reads the Kafka topic, validates the data, creates or updates tables if needed, and writes records into Delta tables.\par\par This is called CDC, or Change Data Capture.\par\par In this project:\par - Source system: MySQL database\par - Transport system: Kafka\par - Processing platform: Azure Databricks\par - Storage format: Delta Lake\par - Governance layer: Unity Catalog\par - Deployment model: Databricks Asset Bundle (DAB)\par\par\pard\fs22\b 3. What Databricks is\b0\par Databricks is a cloud-based platform for data engineering, analytics, machine learning, and streaming. It is built on top of Apache Spark, but it gives a much easier and production-friendly experience than managing raw Spark by yourself.\par\par Databricks is used to:\par - process huge amounts of data\par - run ETL and ELT jobs\par - build data pipelines\par - read from Kafka\par - write to Delta Lake\par - perform analytics and reporting\par - automate pipeline workflows\par\par In simple terms: Databricks is where data engineers build, run, and manage data pipelines.\par\par\pard\fs22\b 4. Why Databricks is useful\b0\par Databricks is valuable because it combines several things together:\par - compute engine\par - data processing\par - storage integration\par - Spark-based processing\par - notebooks for development\par - job orchestration\par - governance with Unity Catalog\par - streaming and batch support\par\par It reduces the complexity of managing Spark clusters and infrastructure manually.\par\par\pard\fs22\b 5. Databricks lakehouse architecture\b0\par This project follows a lakehouse architecture. A lakehouse combines:\par - data lake storage\par - warehouse-like analytics\par - governance\par - schema management\par - Delta tables\par - structured data processing\par\par In a lakehouse:\par - raw data is stored in a lake\par - structured data is processed and transformed\par - tables are managed with Delta\par - analytics and reporting can happen on top of them\par\par This is better than old warehouse-only systems because it keeps raw data and curated data in one ecosystem.\par\par\pard\fs22\b 6. The main layers in this project\b0\par This project follows the bronze-silver-gold pattern.\par\par\b Bronze layer\b0\par - raw ingested data\par - closest to the source\par - includes all CDC metadata\par - does not remove raw payload\par - stores all events as captured\par\par\b Silver layer\b0\par - cleaned and standardized data\par - JSON payloads are parsed\par - duplicates are removed\par - the latest version of each record is kept\par - data becomes business-ready\par\par\b Gold layer\b0\par - final curated analytics tables\par - aggregated metrics or feature tables\par - used for reporting and downstream analytics\par\par This is a standard and modern data engineering approach.\par\par\pard\fs22\b 7. What the repo includes\b0\par The project repository contains:\par - databricks.yml\par - README.md\par - ARCHITECTURE.md\par - INFRASTRUCTURE.md\par - config/pipeline_metadata.json\par - src/main.py\par - src/preflight_task.py\par - src/cdc package\par - notebooks\par - scripts\par - tests\par\par Important files:\par - databricks.yml: deployment and job configuration\par - pipeline_metadata.json: pipeline settings\par - src/main.py: entry point\par - src/cdc/kafka_client.py: Kafka connectivity and inspection\par - src/cdc/schema_manager.py: table creation and schema synchronization\par - src/cdc/stream_writer.py: write stream to Delta\par - src/cdc/pipeline.py: orchestration logic\par - notebooks/Bronze To Silver Final - Final Job (Bronze To Silver).py: bronze-to-silver transformation logic\par - tests/test_schema_manager.py: schema logic unit tests\par\par\pard\fs22\b 8. Azure Databricks workspace\b0\par The project was deployed to an Azure Databricks workspace.\par\par A Databricks workspace is the main environment where:\par - notebooks are created\par - jobs are scheduled\par - clusters are managed\par - code is run\par - data is processed\par - users access the platform\par\par In the project:\par - the workspace host URL is configured in databricks.yml\par - the workspace connects to the Databricks environment\par - the repo is deployed to the workspace using Databricks CLI\par\par This shows the project was not only written on a laptop. It was actually deployed and run in a cloud workspace.\par\par\pard\fs22\b 9. Azure resources related to the project\b0\par This project uses Azure infrastructure for the data platform. Even though the code is mostly Python and Databricks logic, the underlying cloud system includes Azure resources.\par\par Main Azure-related concepts:\par - Azure Databricks workspace\par - Azure compute for Databricks clusters\par - Azure storage for data persistence\par - Unity Catalog storage\par - Volumes for checkpoints\par - Azure networking and security boundaries\par - identity and access configuration\par\par The project uses:\par - Unity Catalog volumes for checkpoint paths\par - Delta tables controlled by Databricks catalog and schema\par - cluster compute created on Azure infrastructure\par\par This shows the project is grounded in real cloud data engineering.\par\par\pard\fs22\b 10. What is a cluster in Databricks\b0\par A cluster is the compute environment used to run Spark jobs.\par\par In Databricks, clusters can be:\par - all-purpose clusters\par - job clusters\par - single-node clusters\par - serverless clusters\par\par Your project uses a single-node cluster.\par\par Why single-node cluster? Because the workload is focused and does not require a full multi-node distributed setup. It helps:\par - lower cost\par - faster startup\par - simple execution\par - enough capacity for streaming ingestion\par\par In the project we see:\par - node type: Standard_D2ds_v6\par - workers: 0\par - spark master: local[*]\par - cluster profile: singleNode\par\par This means the driver runs the work locally on one node. It is efficient for a focused streaming pipeline.\par\par\pard\fs22\b 11. What is a notebook in Databricks\b0\par A notebook is an interactive development environment inside Databricks. You can write:\par - Python code\par - SQL queries\par - markdown explanations\par - visualizations\par - Spark logic\par\par Notebooks are useful because:\par - they allow iterative development\par - they are easy to debug\par - they help build transformation logic step by step\par - they can later be turned into production jobs\par\par This project includes notebooks for:\par - Kafka connection validation\par - bronze table validation\par - bronze-to-silver transformation\par - schema and table creation\par - data validation\par\par These notebook files show practical Databricks development.\par\par\pard\fs22\b 12. What are jobs in Databricks\b0\par A job in Databricks is a scheduled or triggered workflow that executes tasks.\par\par In this project, jobs are used to:\par - run ingestion\par - validate the environment\par - start streaming ingestion only if preflight checks pass\par - orchestrate tasks in a proper order\par\par The main job is called mysql_cdc_stream.\par\par This job has:\par - preflight task\par - stream task\par\par The stream task depends on the preflight task. This is important because it makes the pipeline safe and reliable.\par\par\pard\fs22\b 13. DAG in this project\b0\par A DAG means Directed Acyclic Graph. This is a workflow where tasks depend on each other in one direction and cannot loop back.\par\par In this project, the DAG is simple:\par - preflight task checks Kafka and environment readiness\par - stream task runs only if preflight passes\par\par This matters because:\par - we avoid starting the stream when Kafka is down\par - we reduce wasted compute\par - we ensure clean execution\par - we make the pipeline operationally stable\par\par This is a very strong concept in data engineering.\par\par\pard\fs22\b 14. Preflight checks\b0\par Preflight is a validation step before the main job starts.\par\par The project has a preflight task in src/preflight_task.py.\par\par Purpose:\par - verify Kafka is reachable\par - verify the topic and broker connectivity\par - verify the catalog and schema exist\par - check environment variables\par - fail early if required setup is missing\par\par This is important because:\par - if Kafka is down, we do not want to start the stream\par - if the environment is wrong, we want to fail fast\par - it saves time and reduces errors\par\par This is a real production pattern.\par\par\pard\fs22\b 15. DAB: Databricks Asset Bundle\b0\par Databricks Asset Bundle is a deployment method for defining Databricks resources in code.\par\par The file databricks.yml is the main DAB definition. It contains:\par - bundle name\par - variables\par - target environments\par - jobs\par - task definitions\par - cluster configuration\par - workspace host configuration\par\par This project uses DAB to manage:\par - jobs\par - code structure\par - deployment parameters\par - workspace configuration\par - dev and prod targets\par\par Why this is useful:\par - consistent deployments\par - repeatable environments\par - easier CI/CD\par - no manual configuration each time\par - source control for infrastructure\par\par This is a clear demonstration of modern Databricks deployment practices.\par\par\pard\fs22\b 16. Databricks CLI and local development\b0\par This project was created on a laptop and then pushed to Databricks via the Databricks CLI.\par\par This is a good practice because it shows the ability to:\par - develop locally\par - use Git version control\par - deploy to a remote Databricks workspace\par - run jobs in the cloud\par\par Typical commands used:\par - databricks bundle validate\par - databricks bundle deploy\par - databricks bundle run\par\par This demonstrates:\par - local development workflow\par - infrastructure as code\par - deployment knowledge\par - cloud engineering skill\par\par\pard\fs22\b 17. Catalog, schema, and tables\b0\par In Unity Catalog, data is organized like this:\par - catalog\par - schema\par - tables\par\par In this project:\par - catalog: fraudulenttransactionmonitoring\par - schema: bronze\par - table: activity_log_staging\par\par This is how Databricks organizes data in a governed and structured way.\par\par The bronze table stores raw ingested Kafka messages with:\par - business columns\par - raw payload\par - CDC metadata\par - Kafka metadata\par\par This is important because it makes the data traceable and structured.\par\par\pard\fs22\b 18. Schema management and schema drift\b0\par Schema drift means the source system adds or changes columns over time.\par\par This is common in real-world data pipelines. Example:\par - MySQL table adds a new column\par - the upstream system changes the structure\par - the pipeline must adapt without breaking\par\par This project handles that by:\par - checking the incoming Kafka schema\par - comparing it with the target Delta table\par - creating the table if it does not exist\par - adding missing columns if the table already exists\par\par This is a very important concept in production data engineering.\par\par Why it matters:\par - source schemas change\par - pipelines should adapt automatically\par - no hard failure due to missing columns\par - easier to maintain in real systems\par\par\pard\fs22\b 19. Delta Lake and why it matters\b0\par Delta Lake is used in Databricks for reliable tables. It provides:\par - ACID transactions\par - schema evolution\par - merges\par - structured streaming support\par - checkpoints\par - data versioning and time travel\par\par This project writes processed data into Delta tables and uses:\par - mergeSchema = true\par - automatic table creation\par - Delta writeStream logic\par - checkpointing\par\par This is a strong sign that the project understands real lakehouse engineering.\par\par\pard\fs22\b 20. What Kafka does in this project\b0\par Kafka is used as the message broker between the source and Databricks.\par\par Kafka helps by:\par - buffering events\par - decoupling the source and sink\par - handling high volume data\par - allowing event streaming\par - storing messages until consumed\par\par In the project:\par - Debezium reads MySQL changes\par - Kafka receives them\par - Databricks reads from the Kafka topic\par - processing continues even if downstream systems are slightly delayed\par\par Kafka is important because data can be produced faster than it is processed.\par\par\pard\fs22\b 21. CDC and Debezium\b0\par CDC means Change Data Capture. Debezium is a tool designed to monitor database change logs.\par\par In this project:\par - Debezium reads the MySQL binary log\par - captures row-level changes\par - serializes them into JSON\par - publishes them to Kafka\par\par This is the upstream data movement layer.\par\par Important CDC fields in this project:\par - _cdc_op\par - _cdc_ts_ms\par - _cdc_source_db\par - _cdc_source_table\par - _cdc_binlog_file\par - _cdc_binlog_pos\par\par These fields show what happened, when it happened, and where it came from.\par\par\pard\fs22\b 22. What raw payload means\b0\par Raw payload is the complete JSON payload as captured from Kafka.\par\par This is important because:\par - raw data is preserved\par - audits are possible\par - debugging is easier\par - if the schema changes later, the original source data is still available\par\par The project intentionally stores raw payload in bronze tables so that it is never lost. This is a strong engineering practice.\par\par\pard\fs22\b 23. What the project does in the bronze ingestion part\b0\par This is the first pipeline stage. It does the following:\par - checks Kafka connectivity\par - inspects Kafka topic data\par - reads available messages\par - infers column types\par - creates the target table if missing\par - adds missing columns if needed\par - writes the stream to Delta\par\par This is the foundation of the data pipeline.\par\par\pard\fs22\b 24. What the bronze-to-silver transformation does\b0\par This is where real data cleaning and normalization happens. The bronze-to-silver notebook does:\par - reads from the bronze table\par - detects JSON columns\par - parses nested JSON\par - flattens JSON into columns\par - converts timestamps to proper Spark timestamps\par - removes duplicate records by primary key\par - handles delete events correctly\par - merges the latest valid version into silver\par\par The purpose is to convert raw and messy data into clean, business-useful data.\par\par\pard\fs22\b 25. Why deduplication matters\b0\par CDC often causes duplicate or repeated events due to retries, replays, or reprocessing.\par\par The silver layer uses:\par - primary key matching\par - row_number over a window\par - latest data by _cdc_ts_ms and _kafka_offset\par - Delta merge logic\par\par This ensures:\par - only the latest state is kept\par - duplicate rows are avoided\par - data remains correct\par\par This is a real-world production requirement.\par\par\pard\fs22\b 26. What is Delta merge\b0\par Delta merge is an advanced data operation used to update or insert records based on matching keys.\par\par In this project:\par - data is merged into the silver table by id\par - if the incoming record is newer, it updates the row\par - if the incoming row is a delete event, it removes the matching row\par - if the row does not exist, it inserts it\par\par This is a very good example of CDC-friendly data handling.\par\par\pard\fs22\b 27. What is checkpointing\b0\par Checkpointing means Databricks remembers where a streaming job stopped.\par\par This is important for:\par - restarting after failures\par - no data loss\par - maintaining progress in streaming pipelines\par - exactly-once or at-least-once processing behavior\par\par Your project stores checkpoints in Unity Catalog volume paths. This is very important in production streaming pipelines.\par\par\pard\fs22\b 28. Data quality and validation\b0\par This project includes validation logic and testing to ensure the data is correct.\par\par Examples:\par - check if Kafka is reachable\par - check if topics are readable\par - verify schema\par - validate bronze table output\par - validate duplicate keys\par - assert no duplicates in the silver final table\par\par This shows that the project is not only moving data; it is also controlling quality.\par\par\pard\fs22\b 29. Unit testing in this project\b0\par The repository includes tests for schema manager and data logic.\par\par This matters because:\par - logic must be tested\par - schema inference must be trusted\par - type conversion should be verified\par - schema drift logic should be validated\par\par The tests help catch errors before production deployment. This is a sign of maturity as a data engineer.\par\par\pard\fs22\b 30. What is storage in Databricks\b0\par In Databricks, storage is usually separate from compute.\par\par The project uses:\par - Delta tables\par - Unity Catalog managed storage\par - volumes\par - checkpoint storage\par - Azure storage integration\par\par The main point is: Databricks compute is separate from storage. This keeps the architecture scalable and flexible.\par\par\pard\fs22\b 31. What are volumes in Databricks\b0\par Volumes are storage locations inside Unity Catalog.\par\par They are used for:\par - file storage\par - checkpoints\par - non-tabular files\par - supporting files\par\par In this project, the checkpoint path is stored in a Unity Catalog volume. This is a good example of proper operational data engineering.\par\par\pard\fs22\b 32. Security and governance\b0\par Databricks supports governance through Unity Catalog. This includes:\par - catalog, schema, and table security\par - permissions and access control\par - centralized metadata\par - easier auditing\par\par This project uses Unity Catalog to organize and govern the data. That is an important part of modern cloud data platforms.\par\par\pard\fs22\b 33. What are the Azure and Databricks deployment steps\b0\par The project demonstrates a proper cloud workflow:\par - code written locally\par - Git version control\par - deployment configuration in Databricks Asset Bundle\par - Databricks workspace target defined in YAML\par - CLI deployment to the cloud\par - job execution in the Azure environment\par\par This is exactly how data engineering teams work in production systems.\par\par\pard\fs22\b 34. Why this project is valuable to explain\b0\par This project shows that you understand:\par - Databricks fundamentals\par - Azure Databricks deployment\par - Spark and streaming\par - Kafka and CDC\par - Delta Lake\par - Unity Catalog\par - Databricks jobs and DAGs\par - DAB deployment model\par - schema evolution\par - data quality checks\par - transformations\par - pipeline orchestration\par - local-to-cloud deployment with CLI\par\par This is much more than a beginner project.\par\par\pard\fs22\b 35. Final summary\b0\par This project is a complete data engineering solution showing real-world thinking.\par\par It combines:\par - source ingestion\par - streaming transport\par - Kafka-based CDC\par - Azure Databricks environment\par - cluster configuration\par - jobs and DAG orchestration\par - preflight validation\par - Delta tables\par - bronze-silver-gold architecture\par - schema management\par - deployment through Databricks Asset Bundle\par - local development plus cloud deployment through CLI\par - governance with Unity Catalog\par - unit testing and validation\par\par This is exactly the kind of project that shows serious Databricks and Azure data engineering capability.\par\par\pard\fs22\b 36. Simple interview explanation\b0\par I built a real-time CDC ingestion pipeline on Azure Databricks. It reads MySQL change events using Debezium, sends them into Kafka, and ingests them into Databricks using Spark structured streaming. The raw events are stored in a bronze Delta table, then transformed into a cleaned silver layer, and future analytics layers can be built on top. I used Databricks Asset Bundles to deploy the project in a reproducible way, with preflight validation before the streaming job starts. The pipeline handles schema drift automatically, stores raw payloads for auditability, and uses Delta merge logic for reliable updates and deletes. This project demonstrates my understanding of Databricks fundamentals, Azure cloud deployment, streaming data engineering, governance with Unity Catalog, and production-grade data pipeline design.\par\par\pard\fs22\b 37. Skills you are showing with this project\b0\par This project demonstrates that you know:\par - cloud data engineering\par - Azure Databricks\par - Spark structured streaming\par - Kafka CDC\par - Delta Lake\par - Unity Catalog\par - Databricks jobs and DAGs\par - DAB deployment model\par - schema evolution\par - data quality checks\par - transformations\par - pipeline orchestration\par - local-to-cloud deployment with CLI\par\par This is a strong set of practical skills.\par\pard\fs22\b End of document\b0\par }